+title: Universal workflow — the tooling's three execution surfaces (developer shell · CI check · bot commit)
+verdict: |
+  Verdict (2026-10-16, branch claude/cloud-run-bigquery-pipeline-e94ziy at d18a95f, 42 commits since 2026-10-05): the five reusable
+  skills — aider-scaffold with its diff ledger, tree-f-visualizer, pass-sequence, regen-workflow — ran on every building block of
+  CloudBQ and were installed unchanged in IC-DBT-BQ. They execute on three surfaces. D, the developer's shell on a clone, is the only
+  surface that creates: it writes the recipe, applies the block (aider when an LLM key is present, by hand otherwise — in this project
+  aider never answered, 17 commits carry "Scaffolded-by: hand (no LLM key)"), verifies, commits with trailers, appends the ledger,
+  regenerates the tree, rebuilds the workbook, refreshes the README index. C, the CI job "verify" on every push and pull request,
+  re-runs the same generators in check mode and fails when a committed artefact is stale (27 ledger rows for 42 commits, the 15 others
+  being ledger-only commits the ledger skips by design). B, the bot, is the one surface that commits without a person: a regen workflow
+  runs one generator and commits what changed as github-actions[bot] with a "Regenerated-by:" trailer that the ledger skips. The README
+  index is the one layer that is Parallel across all three (same script, same arguments, same output); everything else is Conditional
+  on "same commit" or Divergent because only the developer surface creates and only the bot surface commits unattended. Remediation
+  rows U01–U09 record what this project corrected on the way: the hand path when no key exists, a tree check that failed only in CI,
+  a ledger check that needed full history, a workbook that is never byte-reproducible, a YAML pushed unparsed, and four review findings
+  on the GitHub Actions skill fixed before the push.
+legend: |
+  Legend: Parity column colours — green Parallel, yellow Conditional, orange Divergent. Layers U0–U11 are the stages of the universal
+  loop (install → recipe → apply → verify → commit → ledger → tree → workbook → index → generate workflows → review → push); Pipeline
+  steps are the same loop as the operator runs it, in order, with the one LIVE gate (the push); Remediation rows U01–U09 are what
+  CloudBQ corrected between 2026-10-05 and 2026-10-16, each with the commit or test that holds the fix. Source: the repository on
+  branch claude/cloud-run-bigquery-pipeline-e94ziy at d18a95f; the same files live in IC-DBT-BQ at 250dc6d.
+surfaces:
+  D: D · Developer shell on a clone (python3, git, bash; aider when an LLM key is present)
+  C: C · CI — .github/workflows/ci.yml job "verify" on push and pull_request (check mode, never writes)
+  B: B · Bot — a regen workflow (.github/workflows/regenerate.yml callers) running one generator and committing as github-actions[bot]
+motions:
+  - motion: D · Developer shell on a clone
+    acquire: git clone; the skills are files under .claude/skills/ in the clone (copied once into a new repository)
+    ubuntu: 'python3 (standard library; PyYAML, openpyxl, pytest, nbformat for the generators and tests), git, bash; aider 0.86 with an LLM key, otherwise the hand path'
+    driver: the person runs each command in order (the loop in UNIVERSAL_WORKFLOW.md); nothing is scheduled
+    state: the working tree and the local commits until the push; .aider* history files (gitignored)
+    assessment: 'The only surface that creates (recipe, block, annotations, spec); everything downstream checks or re-derives what it wrote. Its weak spot is forgetting a regeneration step — which is exactly what C catches.'
+  - motion: C · CI check (ci.yml "verify")
+    acquire: actions/checkout with fetch-depth 0 (the ledger check walks the whole history)
+    ubuntu: 'ubuntu-latest, Python 3.12 (setup-python), Node 22 (setup-node), pip install of the function requirements + pyyaml openpyxl pytest nbformat'
+    driver: on push and pull_request, every commit
+    state: none — the job writes to /tmp only and never commits
+    assessment: 'Cheap and exhaustive: the same generators in --check / --verify mode, the test suite, bash -n and node --check. It cannot fix anything; it tells the developer which regeneration they skipped. The workbook is verified from its YAML, not compared byte-wise (openpyxl output carries a timestamp).'
+  - motion: B · Bot commit (regen workflow)
+    acquire: actions/checkout (fetch-depth as the caller asks; persist-credentials so the step can push)
+    ubuntu: 'ubuntu-latest, Python/Node as the caller asks; the generator named in the caller''s run: input; assets/commit_if_changed.sh embedded verbatim'
+    driver: 'push to main and workflow_dispatch (update-readme-index.yml); any trigger the generator writes — schedule, pull_request (check mode forced)'
+    state: a commit on the branch as github-actions[bot] with a Regenerated-by trailer, or "No changes to commit"
+    assessment: 'The one surface that commits unattended, so it is the most constrained: one generator, one commit step, a change check, a trailer the ledger skips, a guard against re-running on its own commit, a push that retries only on a race. Today it owns the README index; the tree and the workbook are its prospective next consumers.'
